From bab60fa55a3b3fa6874c5740f31bfbddd4071337 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Udhayveer Singh <udhayveersingh@Udhayveer.local>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 00:49:30 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] Carry the tunnel over WireGuard so it survives a change of
 public address

The LAN gateway on the target network moves traffic between WAN uplinks. Every
TCP flow dies at each move, the HTTP/2 MASQUE flow included, and a new MASQUE
session resets every connection inside it. A WireGuard session is not tied to
the client's address: the next packet from the new address carries it on.

wgtun is nativetun over WireGuard, from a curve25519 registration. Two things
make it work on that network. WARP's client id goes in the reserved bytes of
every message. And a few packets the peer ignores go out ahead of the first
packet of a socket and of every handshake, because the firewall there drops a
UDP flow whose first packets are a bare WireGuard handshake.

A watcher keeps the session on a live path. Traffic out and nothing back for
3s moves it to a new socket, since WireGuard's own retry reuses the flow the
network dropped. A handshake older than the peer keeps a session (after sleep,
Go's clock having stopped) starts a new session at once instead of 15s later.
With no handshake at start wgtun exits with status 3 so the caller can fall
back to nativetun; after that it never closes the device, which would take the
caller's routes with it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
